/*
Minimum number of interval reversals to sort a permutation.

Key idea:
For the current permutation p[1..N], define pos[x] = position of value x.
Consider the sequence:
    0, pos[1], pos[2], ..., pos[N], N+1

If pos[i+1] == pos[i] + 1, then values i and i+1 are already adjacent in correct order.
A reversal can increase the number of such "good adjacencies" by at most 2.
Initially let B be the number of breaks:
    breaks = count of i in [0..N] such that next is not previous+1
(where pos[0]=0, pos[N+1]=N+1 conceptually)

To reach sorted order, all breaks must disappear.
Since one reversal can fix at most 2 breaks, answer is at least ceil(breaks/2).

It is also achievable for permutations: the minimum number of reversals equals ceil(breaks/2).
So we only need to count breaks.

Equivalent simpler counting:
Start with breaks = 0
prev = 0
for x = 1..N:
    if pos[x] != prev + 1 -> breaks++
    prev = pos[x]
if prev != N -> breaks++   // compares pos[N] with N+1 sentinel

answer = (breaks + 1) / 2

This is O(N).
*/

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);
        int N = fs.nextInt();
        int[] pos = new int[N + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
            int v = fs.nextInt();
            pos[v] = i;
        }

        int breaks = 0;
        int prevPos = 0; // sentinel position of value 0

        for (int x = 1; x <= N; x++) {
            if (pos[x] != prevPos + 1) breaks++;
            prevPos = pos[x];
        }
        if (prevPos != N) breaks++; // sentinel value N+1 at position N+1

        int ans = (breaks + 1) / 2;
        System.out.println(ans);
    }

    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        FastScanner(InputStream is) {
            in = is;
        }

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        int nextInt() throws IOException {
            int c;
            do {
                c = read();
            } while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);

            int sign = 1;
            if (c == '-') {
                sign = -1;
                c = read();
            }

            int val = 0;
            while (c > ' ') {
                val = val * 10 + (c - '0');
                c = read();
            }
            return val * sign;
        }
    }
}

